Hands On Labs for Java

Assignment Topic: Implementing Inheritance and Polymorphism in Java

Objective:
These assignments aim to help you understand and implement the concepts of inheritance, method overriding, and polymorphism in Java through a practical scenario.

Assignment 1

1.	Define a Base Class:
a.	Create a class called Employee with the following attributes and methods:
i.	Attributes: id ,name , salary
ii.	Methods: toString(), calculateSalary()
2.	Define Derived Classes:
a.	Create two derived classes PermanentEmployee and ConsultantEmployee that inherit from Employee.
b.	The PermanentEmployee class should have additional attributes like basic, hra, da.
c.	The ConsultantEmployee class should have additional attributes like dailyRate and totalDays.
3.	Implement Method Overriding:
a.	Override the toString( ) and calculateSalary( ) methods in PermanentEmployee and ConsultantEmployee classes to provide specific implementations for each type of employee.
4.	Demonstrate Polymorphism:
a.	Create an array of Employee objects and populate it with instances of PermanentEmployee and ConsultantEmployee.
b.	Iterate through the array and call the toString( ) and calculateSalary ( ) methods for each object to demonstrate polymorphic behavior.
5.	Test the Classes:
a.	Write a main class called EmployeeRunner to test your class hierarchy.
b.	Create instances of PermanentEmployee and ConsultantEmployee, assign values to their attributes, and call their methods to ensure everything is functioning correctly.

Assignment 2

1.	Define an Abstract Base Class:
a.	Create an abstract class called Shape with the following attributes and methods:
i.	Attributes: color, area
ii.	Abstract Methods: area( ), perimeter( )
iii.	Non-Abstract Method: toString( )
2.	Define Derived Classes:
a.	Create the following derived classes that extend the Shape abstract class:
i.	Square: Additional attributes: sideLength. Implement the area() and perimeter() methods.
ii.	Circle: Additional attributes: radius. Implement the area() and perimeter() methods.
iii.	Rectangle: Additional attributes: length, width. Implement the area() and perimeter() methods.
3.	Implement Method Overriding:
a.	Override the area() and perimeter() methods in Square, Circle, and Rectangle classes to provide specific implementations for each shape.
4.	Demonstrate Polymorphism:
a.	Create an array of Shape objects and populate it with instances of Square, Circle, and Rectangle.
b.	Iterate through the array and call the area(), perimeter(), and toString() methods for each object to demonstrate polymorphic behavior.
5.	Test the Classes:
a.	Write a main class called ShapeDemo to test your class hierarchy.
b.	Create instances of Square, Circle, and Rectangle, assign values to their attributes, and call their methods to ensure everything is functioning correctly.

Assignment 3

1.	Define an Interface:
a.	Create an interface called Payment with the following methods:
b.	void makePayment(double amount);
c.	void displayPaymentDetails();
2.	Implement the Interface in Classes:
a.	Create the following classes that implement the Payment interface:
i.	CreditCardPayment: Additional attributes: cardNumber, expiryDate. Implement the makePayment(double amount) and displayPaymentDetails() methods.
ii.	PayPalPayment: Additional attributes: email, transactionId. Implement the makePayment(double amount) and displayPaymentDetails() methods.
iii.	BankTransferPayment: Additional attributes: accountNumber, bankName. Implement the makePayment(double amount) and displayPaymentDetails() methods.
3.	Demonstrate Polymorphism:
a.	Create an array of Payment objects and populate it with instances of CreditCardPayment, PayPalPayment, and BankTransferPayment.
b.	Iterate through the array and call the makePayment(double amount) and displayPaymentDetails() methods for each object to demonstrate polymorphic behavior.
4.	Test the Classes:
a.	Write a main class called PaymentDemo to test your class hierarchy.
b.	Create instances of CreditCardPayment, PayPalPayment, and BankTransferPayment, assign values to their attributes, and call their methods to ensure everything is functioning correctly.

